## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a Kubernetes-native control plane for GPU-backed ML workloads, balancing quotas, preemption, and data access while preserving isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kubernetes Operators and CRDs
- GPU scheduling with taints, tolerations, and resource requests
- Priority, preemption and multi-tenant quotas
- Data locality and streaming of dataset shards
- Observability, auditing, and lifecycle finalizers

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler loop for GpuTrainJob
while (true) {
  const jobs = listGpuTrainJobs();
  for (const j of jobs) {
    if (!quotaOk(j)) continue;
    if (!podExists(j)) {
      createGpuPod(j); // with nodeSelector for j.gpuType and resources
    }
    updateStatus(j, {phase: "Running"});
  }
  await sleep(1000);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate preemption scenarios in tests?
- How would you handle mixed GPU architectures and driver versions in the same cluster?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable governance mechanism spanning policy storage, reconciliation, and enforcement across many namespaces, while preserving auditability and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Namespace-scoped policy governance
- Central policy store with versioning
- Auto-generation of RBAC Roles, ResourceQuota, and LimitRange
- Real-time enforcement via admission webhook
- Drift detection and safe rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: reconcile NamespaceGuardPolicy and apply RBAC/limits across a namespace
async function reconcile(ns) {
  const policy = fetchPolicy(ns);
  if (!policy) return;
  ensureRBAC(ns, policy.rbac);
  ensureQuota(ns, policy.quota);
  ensureLimits(ns, policy.limits);
  audit(ns, policy.version);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this at scale across thousands of namespaces?
- How would you handle policy propagation latency and eventual consistency?
